\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\ul\b\f0\fs24\'b6 Verse 1\cf0\ulnone\b0 
\par 1. I met the good Shepherd
\par But now on the plain,
\par As homeward He carried
\par His lost one again.
\par I marvelled how gently
\par His burden He bore;
\par And, as He passed by me,
\par I knelt to adore.
\par 
\par \cf1\ul\b\'b6 Verse 2\cf0\ulnone\b0 
\par 2. O Shepherd, good Shepherd,
\par Thy wounds they are deep;
\par The wolves have sore hurt Thee,
\par In saving Thy sheep;
\par Thy raiment all over
\par With crimson is dyed;
\par And what is this rent
\par They have made in Thy side?
\par 
\par \cf1\ul\b\'b6 Verse 3\cf0\ulnone\b0 
\par 3. Ah me! How the thorns
\par Have entangled Thy hair,
\par And cruelly riven
\par That forehead so fair;
\par How feebly Thou drawest
\par Thy faltering breath;
\par And lo, on Thy face
\par Is the shadow of death.
\par 
\par \cf1\ul\b\'b6 Verse 4\cf0\ulnone\b0 
\par 4. O Shepherd, good Shepherd!
\par And is it for me
\par This grievous affliction
\par Has fallen on Thee?
\par Ah, then let me strive,
\par For the love Thou hast borne,
\par To give Thee no longer
\par Occasion to mourn!\f1 
\par \cf1\ul\b 
\par }

